\ This file provides persistency for BLOCK/Screen facility     
\ The first 512 bytes of this file aren't used by BLOCK.       
\                                                              
( BLOCK system - introduction )                                 (  This Forth System comes with a 16 MBytes file named          (  !Blocks-64.bin that provides a BLOCK-like mass storage       (  system to hold 32767 BLOCKs or 16383 Screens that            (  can be edited using the "Full Screen Editor" utility         (  available after you type NEEDS EDIT                          (  Each BLOCK is 512 bytes and each Screen is 1 KByte long      (  and can store text in 16 lines x 64 columns way or can       (  be used as a virtual-memory area where you can               (  persistently store anything you like.                        (  You are allowed to use your own file but remember that       (  BLOCKs from #8 to #17 are reserved for the standard          (  error messages and should remain such.                       (  Screen #11 is used by AUTOEXEC and can be modified for       (  your purposes.                                                                                                               ( Error messages    from -31 to -17 )                           was not defined by CREATE.                                      Obsolescent feature.                                            Compiler nesting.                                               User interrupt.                                                 Invalid recursion.                                              Loop parameters unavailable.                                    Return stack imbalance.                                         Invalid numeric argument.                                       Address alignment exception.                                    Control structure mismatch.                                     Unsupported operation.                                          Write to a read-only location.                                  Definition name too long.                                       Parsed string overflow.                                         Pictured numeric output string overflow.                        ( Error messages    from -15 to -1  )                           Invalid FORGET.                                                 is a compile-only word.                                         is undefined.                                                   Argument type mismatch.                                         Result out of range.                                            Division by zero.                                               Invalid memory address.                                         Dictionary full.                                                DO loops nested too deeply.                                     Return stack is empty.                                          Return stack full.                                              Stack is empty.                                                 Stack full.                                                     Aborted with message.                                           Aborted.                                                        is undefined.                                                   Stack is empty.                                                 Dictionary full.                                                No such a line.                                                 has already been defined.                                       Invalid stream.                                                 No such a block.                                                Stack full.                                                     Old dictionary is full.                                         Tape error.                                                     Wrong array index.                                              Invalid floating point.                                         Heap full.                                                      Division by zero.                                               Patching the wrong definition.  