                                                             ␀                                                               ␀                                                               ␀                                                               ␀                                                               ␀                                                               ␀                                                               \ Brodie - Starting FORTH - Ch. 10 - I/O and You                                                                                \    |       |       |       |       |       |                    : "LABEL"                                                       ." REJECT  SMALL   MEDIUM  LARGE   XTRA LRGERROR   " ;                                                                        ' "LABEL"  >BODY  3 +                                           16 + 8 TYPE         \ MEDIUM                                                                                                      : LABEL  8 *  ['] "LABEL" >BODY 3 + + 8 TYPE SPACE ;                                                                            : LABEL  0 MAX 5 MIN  LABEL ;                                 ␀                                                               ␀                                                               ␀                                                               ␀                                                               \ Brodie - Starting FORTH - Ch. 10 - I/O and You                \                                                               \ In vForth a BLOCK is 512 bytes (instead of 1024) and          \ two BLOCKS forms a Screen. To access a Screen' BLOCKs         \ you must simply double the Screen number.                     \                                                               \ Screen #n uses BLOCKs  2n and 2n+1                            \                                                               \ To print the first line of this Screen you can use:           \                                                                 CR  879 2*    BLOCK  64 -TRAILING TYPE                        \                                                               \ but see what does the following:                              \                                                                 CR  879 2* 1+ BLOCK  64 -TRAILING TYPE                        ␀                                                               \ Brodie - Starting FORTH - Ch. 10 - I/O and You                \ Buzzphrases Generator - ver 1.                                  needs CHOOSE                                                    : buzzword  ( n -- )                                              10 CHOOSE 2+  881 (LINE) DROP + 20 -TRAILING TYPE ;           : 1adj    0  buzzword ;                                         : 2adj   20  buzzword ;                                         : noun   40  buzzword ;                                         : phrase     1adj SPACE 2adj SPACE noun ;                       : paragraph  CR  ." By using "                                    phrase    ."  coordinated with "                                phrase    ."  it is possible for even the most "                phrase    ."  to function as "                                  phrase    ."  within the constraint of "                        phrase    ." ." CR ;                                                                                                        \ Brodie - Starting FORTH - Ch. 10 - I/O and You                \ Buzzphrases Generator - ver 1.                                integrated          management          criteria                total               organization        flexibility             systematized        monitored           capability              parallel            reciprocal          mobility                functional          digital             programming             responsive          logistical          concepts                optimal             transitional        time phasing            synchronized        incremental         projections             compatible          third generation    hardware                qualified           policy              through-put             partial             decision            engineering                                                                                                                                                                                                             \ Brodie - Starting FORTH - Ch. 10 - I/O and You                \                                                               \ Screen #n uses BLOCKs  2n and 2n+1                            : SCREENS ( count -- )                                             SCR @ +  SCR @  DO  I LIST  KEY DROP                            LOOP ;                                                       \                                                               : BLOCKS  ( count -- )                                             CR 2/ SCR @ +  SCR @  DO                                          I 2*    BLOCK 512 TYPE                                          I 2* 1+ BLOCK 512 TYPE                                          KEY DROP                                                      LOOP ;                                                       ␀                                                               ␀                                                                                                                               \ Brodie - Starting FORTH - Ch. 10 - I/O and You                \                                                               \ Screen #n uses BLOCKs  2n and 2n+1                            : SCREENS ( count -- )                                             SCR @ +  SCR @  DO  I LIST                                        KEY 13 = IF LEAVE THEN                                        LOOP ;                                                       \                                                               NEEDS TEXT \ accepts text to PAD as counted-string              CREATE MY-NAME  40 ALLOT                                        : I'M   BL TEXT  PAD MY-NAME 40 CMOVE ;                         \                                                               : GREET  ." HELLO, " MY-NAME COUNT TYPE                                  ." , I SPEAK FORTH." ;                                                                                                                                                                 \ Brodie - Starting FORTH - Ch. 10 - I/O and You                \ Form love letter                                              VARIABLE NAME 12 ALLOT    VARIABLE EYES 10 ALLOT                VARIABLE ME   12 ALLOT    NEEDS TEXT                            : VITALS   [CHAR] , TEXT  PAD NAME 14 CMOVE                                [CHAR] , TEXT  PAD EYES 12 CMOVE                                       1 TEXT  PAD  ME  14 CMOVE ;                   : LETTER   CLS                                                       ." DEAR " NAME COUNT TYPE  [CHAR] , EMIT                     CR ." I GO TO HEAVEN WHENEVER I SEE YOUR DEEP "                              EYES COUNT TYPE ." EYES.  CAN "                    CR ." YOU GO TO THE MOVIES FRIDAY? "                            CR       30 SPACES  ." LOVE,"                                   CR       30 SPACES  ME COUNT TYPE                               CR ." P.S.  WEAR SOMETHING " EYES COUNT TYPE                       ." TO SHOW OFF THOSE EYES! " ;                             \ Brodie - Starting FORTH - Ch. 10 - I/O and You                \                                                               NEEDS TEXT   NEEDS EXPECT                                       \                                                               : GREET  CR ." WHAT'S YOUR NAME? " S0 @  40 EXPECT                     0 >IN !  1 TEXT  CR ." HELLO, "                              PAD COUNT TYPE  ." , I SPEAK FORTH. " ;                     \                                                               \ vForth has (COMPARE) that is quite similar to -TEXT           : -TEXT        ( a1 u a2 -- f )                                   SWAP (COMPARE) ;                                              ␀                                                               ␀                                                               ␀                                                               ␀                                                               ␀                                                               \ Brodie - Starting FORTH - Ch. 10 - I/O and You                \ Problem 1. Enter some famous quotation into an available      \ screen# say 887. Now define a word caled CHANGE which takes   \ two ASCII values and change all the occurrence within such a  \ screen of the first character into the second character.      \ For example:  CHAR a CHAR A CHANGE                                                                                            : CHANGE-BLK ( c1 c2 n -- )                                       >R SWAP R> BLOCK 512 OVER + SWAP                                DO                                                                2DUP I C@ = IF I C! ELSE DROP THEN                            LOOP 2DROP ;                                                  : CHANGE ( c1 c2 -- )                                             2DUP 999 2*    CHANGE-BLK                                            999 2* 1+ CHANGE-BLK ;                                                                                                   \ Brodie - Starting FORTH - Ch. 10 - I/O and You                And therefore as a stranger give it welcome.                    There are more things in heaven and earth, Horatio,             Than are dreamt of in your philosophy. But come;                Here, as before, never, so help you mercy,                      How strange or odd soe'er I bear myself,                        As I perchance hereafter shall think meet                       To put an antic disposition on,                                 That you, at such times seeing me, never shall,                 With arms encumber'd thus, or this headshake,                   Or by pronouncing of some doubtful phrase,                      As 'Well, well, we know,' or 'We could, an if we would,'        Or 'If we list to speak,' or 'There be, an if they might,'      Or such ambiguous giving out, to note                           That you know aught of me: this not to do,                      So grace and mercy at your most need help you, Swear.           \ Brodie - Starting FORTH - Ch. 10 - I/O and You                \ Problem 2. Define a word called FORTUNE which will print a    \ prediction at your terminal, such as "You will receive a      \ good news in the mail". The prdiction should be chosen at     \ random from a list of sixteen or fewer predictions.           NEEDS CHOOSE                                                    : FORTUNE   CR  8 CHOOSE 64 *  888 2* 1+ BLOCK +                            64 -TRAILING TYPE SPACE ;   EXIT ( ! )               A wonderful surprise awaits you soon.                           Embrace the new opportunities ahead.                            Your kindness will return multiplied.                           Success is closer than you think.                               Love and joy are entering your life.                            A happy journey begins with one step.                           Great things are coming your way.                               Your hard work will soon be rewarded.                          \ Brodie - Starting FORTH - Ch. 10 - I/O and You                \ Problem 3. According to Oriental legend, [... snipped ...]    \ Here is the orde of the cycle:                                \    Rat Ox Tiger Rabbit Dragon Snake                           \    Horse Ram Monkey Cock Dog Boar                             \ Write a word called .ANIMAL that types the name of the        \ animal corresponding to its position in the cycle as          \ listed here e.g. 0 .ANIMAL RAT ok  [... snipped ...]          NEEDS EXPECT   CREATE ANIMALS    ," RAT   OX    TIGER RABBITDRAGONSNAKE HORSE RAM   MONKEYCOCK  DOG   BOAR  "                   : .ANIMAL 6 *  ['] ANIMALS 4 +  + 6 -TRAILING TYPE ;            : (JUNEESHEE) ( yr -- )                                            ." YOU WERE BORN IN THE YEAR OF THE "                           1900 - 12 MOD .ANIMAL  [CHAR] . EMIT  CR ;                   : JUNEESHEE  CR ." IN WHAT YEAR WERE YOU BORN? "                  S0 @ 4 EXPECT  0 >IN  ! 1 WORD  NUMBER +- CR (JUNEESHEE) ;    \ Brodie - Starting FORTH - Ch. 10 - I/O and You                \ Problem 4. Rewrite the definition of LETTER that appears      \ in this chapter so that it uses names and pesonal descriptions\ that have been edited into a block, rather than entered into  \ character arrays. In this way, you can keep a file on many    \ "prospects" and produce a letter for any one person with the  \ appropriate descriptions, just by supplying an argument to    \ LETTER, as in 1 LETTER                                        \ Now define LETTERS so that it prints one letter for each      \ person in your file.                                          \                                                               : NAME   64 *  891 2* 1+ BLOCK +      24 -TRAILING TYPE ;       : HAIR   64 *  891 2* 1+ BLOCK + 24 + 20 -TRAILING TYPE ;       : EYES   64 *  891 2* 1+ BLOCK + 44 + 20 -TRAILING TYPE ;       -->  \ continue compiling to the next Screen                                                                                    \ Brodie - Starting FORTH - Ch. 10 - I/O and You                : LETTER   CR CR DUP DUP                                             ." DEAR " NAME ." ," CR                                    CR ." YOU'RE THE ONLY ONE FOR ME.  LET ME RUN MY FINGERS "      CR ." THROUGH YOUR NICE " HAIR ."  HAIR.  LET ME LOOK INTO "    CR ." YOUR DEEP " EYES ."  EYES. " ;                            : LETTERS  4 0 DO I LETTER LOOP ; EXIT                          \                                                               LATICIA                 BLACK               BROWN               ALICE                   BLONDE              BLUE                STACEY                  BROWN               HAZEL               BARBARA                 BROWN               GREEN                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               \ Brodie - Starting FORTH - Ch. 10 - I/O and You                \ Problem 5. In this exercise you will create and use a         \ virtual array, that is, an array which resides on the disk    \ but which is referenced like a memory-resident array (with    \ @ and !).                                                     \ Firt select an unused block in your range of assigned blo-    \ cks.  There can be no text on this block; binary data will    \ be stored in it. Put this block number in a variable. Then    \ define an access  word which accepts a cell subscript from    \ the stack, then computes the block number corresponding to    \ this subscripted cell. This access word should also call      \ UPDATE. Test your work so far.                                VARIABLE #START    15000 2* #START !                            : ELEMENT ( n -- a ) 2* 512 /MOD #START @ + BLOCK + UPDATE ;    : INIT-ARRAY ( -- ) 500 0 DO  I  I ELEMENT   ! LOOP ;           : .ARRAY ( n -- )  0 DO  CR I . SPACE  I ELEMENT ?  LOOP ;      \ Brodie - Starting FORTH - Ch. 10 - I/O and You                \ Problem 5. (continued from previous screen)                   \ Next use the first cell as a count of how many data items     \ are stored in the array. Define a word PUT which will         \ store a value into the next available cell of the array.      \ Define a display routine which will print the stored          \ in the array.                                                 \                                                               VARIABLE #START    15000 2* #START !                            : AVAILABLE  ( -- addr ) #START @ BLOCK UPDATE ;                0 AVAILABLE !                                                   : ELEMENT ( index -- addr )                                       1+ CELLS B/BUF /MOD #START @ + BLOCK + UPDATE ;               : PUT ( value -- ) AVAILABLE @ ELEMENT ! 1 AVAILABLE +! ;       : SHOW ( -- ) AVAILABLE @ 0 DO CR I . I ELEMENT ? LOOP ;        -->                                                             \ Brodie - Starting FORTH - Ch. 10 - I/O and You                \ Problem 5. (continued from previous screen)                   \ Now use this virtual array facility to define a word          \ ENTER which will accept pairs of numbers and store them in    \ the array. Finally, define TABLE to print the data entered    \ above, eight numbers per line.                                \                                                               NEEDS U.R                                                       \                                                               : ENTER ( value1 value2 -- ) SWAP PUT PUT ;                     \                                                               : TABLE AVAILABLE @ ?DUP IF                                       CR 0 DO  I 8 MOD  0= IF CR THEN                                          I ELEMENT @  8 U.R  LOOP CR                            THEN ;                                                                                                                        \ Brodie - Starting FORTH - Ch. 10 - I/O and You                \ Problem 5. (continued from previous screen)                   VARIABLE #START    15000 2* #START !                            : AVAILABLE  ( -- addr ) #START @ BLOCK UPDATE ;                0 AVAILABLE !                                                   : ELEMENT ( index -- addr )                                       1+ CELLS B/BUF /MOD #START @ + BLOCK + UPDATE ;               : PUT ( value -- ) AVAILABLE @ ELEMENT ! 1 AVAILABLE +! ;       : SHOW ( -- ) AVAILABLE @ 0 DO CR I . I ELEMENT ? LOOP ;                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        \ Brodie - Starting FORTH - Ch.11 - Extending the Compiler      \ Defining Words: a word whose job is to define new words.      : CONSTANT  CREATE , DOES> @ ;                                  5 CONSTANT FIVE                                                 FIVE .                                                          \                                                               : CHARACTERS                                                        CREATE        \ creates a new dictionary entry.                 DUP , ALLOT   \ compiles the count n, and the n bytes         DOES>           \ marks the beginning of run-time code            DUP           \ copies the pfa.                                 CELL+         \ advances the address to past the count          SWAP @        \ swaps string address with the count         ;                                                               20 CHARACTERS ME                                                                                                                \ Brodie - Starting FORTH - Ch.11 - Extending the Compiler      \ : STRING   CREATE ALLOT  DOES> +  ;                           NEEDS ABORT"                                                    : STRING CREATE DUP , ALLOT                                              DOES> 2DUP @ U< NOT ABORT" RANGE ERROR " + 2+ ;        30 STRING VALVE                                                 6 VALVE C@ .                                                    : ERASED   HERE OVER  ERASE ALLOT ;                             : 0STRING  CREATE ERASED DOES> + ;                              \                                                               \ 2D array                                                      : ARRAY   ( #rows #col -- )                                       CREATE OVER , * ALLOT                                           DOES>  DUP @ ROT * + + 2+ ;                                   4 4 ARRAY BOARD                                                 2 1 BOARD C@                                                    \ Brodie - Starting FORTH - Ch.11 - Compiling Words             : STAR   42 EMIT ;                                              : .ROW   CR 8 0 DO DUP 128 AND                                           IF STAR ELSE SPACE THEN 2* LOOP DROP ;                 : SHAPE  CREATE  8 0 DO C, LOOP                                          DOES> DUP 7 + DO I C@ .ROW -1 +LOOP CR ;               HEX  18 18 3C 5A 99 24 24 24  SHAPE MAN                              81 42 24 18 18 24 42 81  SHAPE EQUIS                            AA AA FE FE 38 38 38 FE  SHAPE CASTLE                      DECIMAL                                                         MAN                                                             EQUIS                                                           CASTLE                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          \ Brodie - Starting FORTH - Ch.11 - Compiling Words             \ IMMEDIATE marks a word to RUN while compiling.                \                                                                 : SAY-HELLO   ." HELLO "  ;  IMMEDIATE                          : GREET  SAY-HELLO  ." I SPEAK FORTH " ;   \ HELLO              GREET                                      \ I SPEAK FORTH    \                                                               \ [COMPILE] postpones an immediate word so it is compiled       \ instead, firing only when the new word later runs:              : GREETING  [COMPILE] SAY-HELLO ." I SPEAK FORTH " ;            GREETING                  \ HELLO I SPEAK FORTH               \ vForth also offers the standard POSTPONE ( NEEDS POSTPONE )   \                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               \ Brodie - Starting FORTH - Ch.11 - Compiling Words             \ COMPILE lays down the NEXT word's xt at run time, letting     \ an immediate helper plant code into the definition it is      \ helping to build.                                             : FOUR-MORE  [ 2 2 + ] LITERAL + ;                              10 FOUR-MORE .                                                  \                                                               : SAY-HELLO  ." HELLO " ;                                       : GREET  [  SAY-HELLO  ]  ." I SPEAK FORTH " ; \ HELLO            GREET  \ I SPEAK FORTH                                        \                                                               : LIST-THIS  [ BLK @ ] LITERAL LIST ;                           \                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               \ Brodie - Starting FORTH - Ch. 11 - Extending the Compiler     \ Problem 1. Define a defining word named LOADED-BY that        \ will define words which load a block when they are            \ executed. Example 6000 LOADED-BY CORRESPONDENCE               : LOADED-BY   CREATE ,  DOES>  @ LOAD ;                                                                                         \ Problem 2. Define a defining word BASED. which will create    \ number output words for specific bases. For example,          \  16 BASED. H.  would define  H.  to be a word which prints    \ the top of the stack in hex but does not permanently change   \ BASE. e.g.  DECIMAL 17 DUP H. .  ->  11 17                    : BASED.   CREATE  31 AND ,                                                DOES> @ BASE @ SWAP BASE ! SWAP . BASE ! ;                                                                                                                                                                                                           \ Brodie - Starting FORTH - Ch. 11 - Extending the Compiler     \ Problem 3. Define a defining word called PLURAL which         \ will take the  xt  of a word such as CR or STAR and           \ create its plural form, such as CRS or STARS. You'll          \ provide PLURAL with the  xt  of the singular word by using    \ tick. For instance the phrase   ' CR PLURALS CRS   will       \ define CRS the same way as though you had defined it          \   : CRS 0 ?DO CR LOOP ;                                       : PLURAL ( adr -- )    CREATE ,                                   DOES> @ SWAP 0 ?DO DUP EXECUTE LOOP DROP ;                    ' CR PLURAL CRS                                                 5 CRS                                                           : BEEP  7 EMIT 20000 0 DO LOOP ;  ' BEEP PLURAL BEEPS           4 BEEPS                                                                                                                                                                                         \ Brodie - Starting FORTH - Ch. 11 - Extending the Compiler     \ Problem 4. The French words for DO and LOOP are TOURNE        \ and RETOURNE. Using the words DO and LOOP, define TOURNE      \ and RETOURNE as French "aliases". Now test them by writing    \ yourself a French loop.                                       : TOURNE [COMPILE] DO ; IMMEDIATE                               : RETOURNE [COMPILE] LOOP ; IMMEDIATE                           : TRY  10 0 TOURNE  I .  RETOURNE ;                             \                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               \ Brodie - Starting FORTH - Ch. 11 - Extending the Compiler     \ Problem 5. The Forth-79 Standard Reference Word Set           \ contains a word called ASCII that can be used to make         \ certain definitions more readable. Instead of using a         \ numeric ASCII code within a definition, such as               \  : STAR 42 EMIT ;  you can use  : STAR ASCII * EMIT ;         \ The word ASCII reads the next character in the input          \ stream, then compiles its ASCII equivalent into the defi-     \ nition as a literal. When the definition of STARS is exe-     \ cuted, the ASCII value is pushed onto the stack.              : ASCII BL WORD 1+ C@ [COMPILE] LITERAL ; IMMEDIATE             : STAR ASCII *  EMIT ;                                                                                                          \ N.B. vForth has a built-in [CHAR] that is equivalent          \      to the above definition of ASCII.                                                                                        \ Brodie - Starting FORTH - Ch. 11 - Extending the Compiler     \ Problem 6. Write a word called LOOPS which will cause the     \ remainder of the input stream, up to the carriage return,     \ to be executed the numbe of times specified by the value      \ on the stack. For example: 7 LOOPS  42 EMITS SPACE            \   7 LOOPS  42 EMITS SPACE                                     \ will print                                                    \   * * * * * * * ok                                            \                                                               : LOOPS  >IN @ SWAP  0 DO  DUP >IN !  INTERPRET  LOOP DROP ;    \                                                               10 LOOPS  CR  30 SPACES  STARS                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  ( UART Raspberry PI Zero - receive )                            HEX                                                             : ?UART-BYTE-READY  ( -- f )  \ non zero when byte ready          UART-TX-PORT  P@  1 AND                                       ;                                                               : UART-RX  ( -- b | 0 )       \ accept a byte                     UART-RX-PORT  P@                                              ;                                                               : UART-WAIT ( b -- )     \ wait for a specific byte or Break      BEGIN                       \ b                                   UART-RX OVER =            \ b f                                 ?TERMINAL OR              \ b f                               UNTIL DROP                  \                                 ;                                                               DECIMAL -->                                                                                                                     ( UART Raspberry PI Zero - receive )                            \ wait for a byte with timeout in ms                            : UART-RX-TIMEOUT ( n -- c | 0 )                                  0 SWAP                      \ 0 n                               1+ 0                        \ 0 n+1 0                           DO                          \ 0                                   ?UART-BYTE-READY IF       \ 0                                     UART-RX                 \ 0 b                                   SWAP DROP               \   b                                   LEAVE                   \   b                                 THEN                      \ 0                                   NOOP                      \      minimum delay                LOOP                        \ 0                               ;                                                               DECIMAL -->                                                                                                                     ( UART Raspberry PI Zero - receive )                            HEX                                                             : UART-WAIT-READY                                                 BEGIN                                                             ?UART-BYTE-READY ?TERMINAL OR                                 UNTIL                                                         ;                                                               \ simple wait for a specific string "SUP> "                     : UART-WAIT-PROMPT  ( -- )                                        [CHAR] S UART-WAIT                                              [CHAR] U UART-WAIT                                              [CHAR] P UART-WAIT                                              [CHAR] > UART-WAIT                                                    BL UART-WAIT                                            ;                                                               DECIMAL -->                                                     ( UART Raspberry PI Zero - receive a block )                    \ Accept from I/O  up to n bytes and store them at a.           CODE UART-RX-BURST ( t a n -- )                                      di                                                              pop     DE|                   \ length counter                  Exx                                                             pop     hl|                   \ address                         pop     de|                   \ timeout                         ldx     bc|  UART-TX-PORT  NN,  \ 133Bh                    HERE                               \              a1                 decx    de|                   \ check timeout      \ 6          ld      a'|  d|                                    \ 4          ora      e|                                        \ 4          jrf     z'|  HOLDPLACE        \              a1 a2 \ 7          -->                                                                                                                        ( UART Raspberry PI Zero - receive a block )                         in(c)   a'|                  \ bit 0               \ 12         rra                          \ non-zero when ready \  4         jrf    nc'|  1 PICK BACK,    \ back to a1          \  7         inc     b'|                  \ RX port 143Bh       \  4         ini                          \ bc goes 133Bh       \ 16         ld      a'| (hl)|                                  \  4         anda     a|                  \ check for 0x00      \  4         jrf     z'|  HOLDPLACE       \            a1 a2 a3 \  7         exX                                                \  4         Decx    DE|                  \ dec length counter  \  6         Ld      A'|  D|                                    \  4         Ora      E|                  \ check for zero      \  4         Exx                                                \  4    -->                                                                                                                             ( UART Raspberry PI Zero - receive a block )                    \ At 115200 Bauds bit duration is 8.68  micro-seconds           \ a 512 bytes-buffer is filled in less than 4.45 ms.            \ The 512 bytes burst-read takes 56832 T-states                 \ plus enter-exit time that at 28 MHz is about 2.03 ms          \ so we emtpy the buffer  2 times faster than it fills up.      \                                                                    jrf    nz'|  1 PICK BACK,     \ repeat until done  \ 10    HERE DISP,    \ a3                                              HERE DISP,    \ a2                                                   DROP     \ a1                                                   exx                                                             ei                                                              jpix                                                            C;  -->                                                                                                                    ( UART Raspberry PI Zero - receive a block )                    HEX                                                             \                                                               CODE  DIS-INT                                                     F3 C,             \ di                                          DD C, E9 C,       \ jp (ix)                                   SMUDGE                                                          \   UART-TX-PORT P@ 1 AND                                       CODE  ENA-INT                                                     FB C,             \ di                                          DD C, E9 C,       \ jp (ix)                                   SMUDGE                                                          \                                                               DECIMAL -->                                                                                                                                                                                     ( UART Raspberry PI Zero - receive a block )                    DECIMAL                                                         : UART-RX-CHUNK ( t a n -- )                                      DIS-INT                          \ t a n                        ROT 0 DO                         \ a n                            UART-TX-PORT P@ 1 AND IF       \ a n f   wait ready               SWAP UART-RX-PORT P@         \ n a b   accept byte              ?DUP 0= IF LEAVE THEN        \ n a b   0x00 quits               OVER C! 1+ SWAP 1-           \ a n     store & incr             DUP 0= IF LEAVE THEN         \ a n     decr counter           THEN                                           